1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,502
For thirty years, this little box held

2
00:00:04,522 --> 00:00:06,371
our birthdays, our weddings,

3
00:00:06,391 --> 00:00:08,844
and every movie we ever rented.

4
00:00:08,864 --> 00:00:11,004
Most people assume there’s film inside.

5
00:00:11,024 --> 00:00:13,696
But there isn’t a single picture on it.

6
00:00:22,246 --> 00:00:24,848
Every frame of film is a tiny photograph.

7
00:00:24,881 --> 00:00:26,835
But hold videotape up to the light,

8
00:00:26,855 --> 00:00:29,320
and all you see is rusty brown.

9
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:31,038
That’s because film stores light,

10
00:00:31,058 --> 00:00:34,160
while VHS tape stores magnetism.

11
00:00:34,910 --> 00:00:36,481
The cassette is a sealed little

12
00:00:36,501 --> 00:00:38,048
machine, with two hundred and

13
00:00:38,068 --> 00:00:40,347
forty-six metres of tape inside.

14
00:00:40,367 --> 00:00:41,914
A little lever in the VCR

15
00:00:41,934 --> 00:00:43,307
feels for this tab.

16
00:00:43,327 --> 00:00:45,281
Snap it off, and the lever finds a hole,

17
00:00:45,301 --> 00:00:47,114
so the machine won’t record.

18
00:00:47,274 --> 00:00:49,228
Nobody can tape over your wedding.

19
00:00:49,248 --> 00:00:51,016
And a lamp in the VCR spots the

20
00:00:51,036 --> 00:00:52,861
clear leader at the end.

21
00:00:54,061 --> 00:00:56,166
Zoom in ten thousand times.

22
00:00:56,186 --> 00:00:57,536
The tape is a plastic ribbon,

23
00:00:57,556 --> 00:00:58,639
thinner than paper,

24
00:00:58,659 --> 00:01:02,075
coated in billions of needles of iron oxide: rust.

25
00:01:02,095 --> 00:01:03,712
Each one is a tiny bar magnet,

26
00:01:03,732 --> 00:01:05,129
pointing any which way.

27
00:01:05,149 --> 00:01:07,554
Play that back, and you get snow.

28
00:01:08,304 --> 00:01:10,142
To record, the VCR uses an

29
00:01:10,162 --> 00:01:11,941
electromagnet with a gap narrower

30
00:01:11,961 --> 00:01:13,543
than a wavelength of light.

31
00:01:13,563 --> 00:01:15,738
Its field flips every needle passing

32
00:01:15,758 --> 00:01:18,013
underneath, freezing the video signal into

33
00:01:18,033 --> 00:01:22,586
stripes: north, south, north, south.

34
00:01:23,336 --> 00:01:26,449
To play it back, the same head listens.

35
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:28,227
Every stripe boundary makes a tiny

36
00:01:28,247 --> 00:01:29,701
pulse of electricity.

37
00:01:29,721 --> 00:01:31,732
Tight stripes mean white;

38
00:01:31,765 --> 00:01:33,856
loose stripes, black.

39
00:01:34,017 --> 00:01:36,550
It’s the same trick as FM radio.

40
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:39,333
You can actually see a recording.

41
00:01:39,367 --> 00:01:41,216
Brush on magnetic developer,

42
00:01:41,236 --> 00:01:43,004
and the hidden pattern appears.

43
00:01:43,024 --> 00:01:45,233
Still no pictures: just stripes,

44
00:01:45,253 --> 00:01:48,238
hundreds of thousands of them, all diagonal.

45
00:01:48,399 --> 00:01:49,795
Why diagonal?

46
00:01:49,815 --> 00:01:53,114
The answer is spinning inside the VCR.

47
00:01:57,014 --> 00:01:57,969
Here’s the problem.

48
00:01:57,989 --> 00:02:00,326
To record video, the head must sweep across the

49
00:02:00,346 --> 00:02:02,671
tape at nearly six metres a second.

50
00:02:02,691 --> 00:02:04,587
At that speed, a two-hour tape would

51
00:02:04,607 --> 00:02:06,294
last forty-two seconds.

52
00:02:06,314 --> 00:02:09,138
Yet VHS tape crawls along at barely

53
00:02:09,158 --> 00:02:11,018
three centimetres a second.

54
00:02:11,768 --> 00:02:12,791
The trick?

55
00:02:12,883 --> 00:02:14,440
Move the heads instead.

56
00:02:14,473 --> 00:02:16,555
Press play, and guide posts wrap the

57
00:02:16,575 --> 00:02:18,401
tape around a slanted drum.

58
00:02:18,421 --> 00:02:21,164
It spins eighteen hundred times a minute,

59
00:02:21,184 --> 00:02:24,032
carrying two heads that sweep across the tape

60
00:02:24,052 --> 00:02:26,422
at twenty-one kilometres an hour.

61
00:02:27,172 --> 00:02:28,870
That slant is the key.

62
00:02:28,890 --> 00:02:31,065
The drum is tipped about six degrees,

63
00:02:31,085 --> 00:02:33,246
so the tape spirals around it.

64
00:02:33,279 --> 00:02:35,233
Every sweep of a head cuts a long

65
00:02:35,253 --> 00:02:37,137
diagonal line across the tape.

66
00:02:37,157 --> 00:02:39,411
It’s called helical scan.

67
00:02:40,161 --> 00:02:42,566
Unroll the tape, and there’s the pattern.

68
00:02:42,657 --> 00:02:45,294
Each stripe is half a TV picture.

69
00:02:45,385 --> 00:02:46,770
Head A lays one,

70
00:02:46,790 --> 00:02:47,908
head B the next,

71
00:02:47,928 --> 00:02:49,253
sixty a second.

72
00:02:49,344 --> 00:02:50,474
Over a two-hour movie,

73
00:02:50,494 --> 00:02:52,575
they cover forty-two kilometres,

74
00:02:52,595 --> 00:02:55,696
on two hundred and forty-six metres of tape.

75
00:02:56,446 --> 00:02:59,224
Notice there are no gaps between stripes.

76
00:02:59,244 --> 00:03:01,430
So how does a head avoid its neighbour?

77
00:03:01,450 --> 00:03:03,032
The two heads are angled in

78
00:03:03,052 --> 00:03:04,483
opposite directions.

79
00:03:04,503 --> 00:03:05,563
On the wrong stripe,

80
00:03:05,583 --> 00:03:08,361
the angles clash and the signal vanishes.

81
00:03:08,381 --> 00:03:10,263
It’s called azimuth recording.

82
00:03:14,163 --> 00:03:16,034
Which brings us to tracking.

83
00:03:16,125 --> 00:03:18,089
Film has sprocket holes.

84
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:20,388
VHS has a control track:

85
00:03:20,479 --> 00:03:22,826
one magnetic pulse per frame.

86
00:03:22,859 --> 00:03:24,905
A servo lines those pulses up with

87
00:03:24,925 --> 00:03:26,066
the spinning drum,

88
00:03:26,086 --> 00:03:29,467
so each head lands right on its stripe.

89
00:03:30,217 --> 00:03:31,880
At least, on your own tapes.

90
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:33,657
A tape from another VCR has its

91
00:03:33,677 --> 00:03:35,689
stripes a few microns off.

92
00:03:35,709 --> 00:03:37,442
The heads stray onto their neighbours,

93
00:03:37,462 --> 00:03:39,182
and you get a band of snow.

94
00:03:39,215 --> 00:03:42,270
Nudge the tracking, and it rolls away.

95
00:03:43,770 --> 00:03:46,258
Even a perfect tape has one flaw.

96
00:03:46,278 --> 00:03:48,800
The heads take turns sixty times a second,

97
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:50,716
switching near the bottom of the picture.

98
00:03:50,736 --> 00:03:52,144
Old TVs hid that.

99
00:03:52,164 --> 00:03:53,375
Digitize a tape, though,

100
00:03:53,395 --> 00:03:55,197
and there it is: a ragged,

101
00:03:55,217 --> 00:03:56,752
wobbling strip.

102
00:03:57,502 --> 00:03:59,466
Colour got the cheap seats.

103
00:03:59,557 --> 00:04:01,929
VHS squeezes it into a narrow band

104
00:04:01,949 --> 00:04:03,148
beneath the brightness,

105
00:04:03,168 --> 00:04:04,889
so reds bleed sideways,

106
00:04:04,909 --> 00:04:06,793
and edges go soft.

107
00:04:06,813 --> 00:04:09,601
It’s the look we still call VHS.

108
00:04:10,351 --> 00:04:13,535
In 1984, VHS Hi‑Fi added two more

109
00:04:13,555 --> 00:04:15,532
heads, writing stereo sound deep into

110
00:04:15,552 --> 00:04:16,798
the magnetic coating.

111
00:04:16,818 --> 00:04:18,145
The video heads then write the

112
00:04:18,165 --> 00:04:20,420
picture on top: sound and picture,

113
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:22,357
one buried beneath the other.

114
00:04:23,807 --> 00:04:26,643
The last VCRs were made in 2016.

115
00:04:26,663 --> 00:04:28,535
But millions of tapes are still out

116
00:04:28,555 --> 00:04:30,532
there: birthdays, weddings,

117
00:04:30,552 --> 00:04:32,599
movie nights, written in rust,

118
00:04:32,619 --> 00:04:35,246
in diagonal stripes thinner than a hair.

119
00:04:35,266 --> 00:04:36,430
So be kind.

120
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:37,764
Rewind.
